    "md5": "d266eaba38acb4d0f8d48e895c88f65d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1980,
    "chunk_text": "Two decades on, Aditya\u2019s quiet blueprint still shapes Sri Lanka\u2019s boutique luxury\n\nLong before boutique villas became the calling card of Sri Lanka\u2019s southern coast, a small circle of friends turned plans for a four-bedroom holiday home on a secluded beach at Rathgama, between Hikkaduwa and Galle, into an all-suite resort that today holds registration number 001 on the Sri Lanka Tourism Development Authority\u2019s (SLTDA) register of boutique hotels.\n\nLast week, Aditya Resort\u2019s founders, staff and a gathering of long-standing partners, many of whom had made the drive down from Colombo, came together at the property to mark its 20th anniversary, in an afternoon that doubled as a reflection on how a passion project helped define a segment now central to the island\u2019s pitch for high-spending travellers.\n\n\u201cWithout your continuous support, we wouldn\u2019t be here,\u201d Aditya Resort Managing Director Husain Dawoodbhoy told the gathering, thanking guests for keeping the resort going for two decades \u201cthrough some good times and some very bad times\u201d.\n\nAditya Co-founder and Chairman Asgi Akbarally, who is also Managing Director of Akbar Brothers, recalled that when the project took shape some 22 years ago, people called the entire undertaking \u201ca mad idea\u201d. What was meant to be a four-bedroom holiday home \u201cturned out to be this\u201d, he observed, crediting his classmate and the resort\u2019s architect, the late Bernard Gomez, who \u201cdecided to break every rule of architecture\u201d.\n\nFollowing a structural review, the team chose to retain just one wall of the old house, Akbarally explained, and it remains the only trace of the building that once stood on the site.\n\nGomez, a Fellow of the Sri Lanka Institute of Architects and principal architect of BGJF Consultancy Services, did not live to see the milestone. Dawoodbhoy asked guests to stand for a minute\u2019s silence in his memory, recalling a warm exchange with the architect the last time they spoke."
