    "chunk_size_bytes": 1842,
    "chunk_text": "Its formula was simple: very few keys, unusually generous suites with private pools, design-led interiors and unscripted personal service on a secluded beach. That formula has since become a familiar template down south, where boutique hotels and private villas now court the same affluent traveller along much of the coast between Galle and Tangalle. UK specialist operator Experience Travel Group, for one, credits Aditya with raising the bar on the island\u2019s early boutique concept.\n\nAs per SLTDA data, the island had 39 registered boutique hotels with 877 rooms in 2025, up from 38 hotels with 835 rooms a year earlier, alongside 50 boutique villas with 353 rooms. Together, those 1,230 rooms account for barely 2 percent of Sri Lanka\u2019s 59,578 registered tourist rooms. Yet the Galle district alone holds 436 of them, or about 35 percent, underscoring how firmly the format has taken root down south. The SLTDA has also acknowledged that a share of the luxury boutique stock operates outside its register, saying in 2024 that it hoped to bring such properties into the formal sector to cater for rising high-end demand.\n\nGomez himself set out the underlying philosophy in 2021, arguing that Sri Lanka\u2019s coastal tourism should embrace a less-is-more approach. He called for plots of roughly an acre carrying a handful of carefully designed units over less than half the land, and buildings no taller than five storeys so as to stay beneath the line of the coastal coconut palms. He also wanted hospitality that leans on local cooking and local character. Conceived some 15 years earlier, Aditya reads as an early built argument for the same view.\n\n\u201cWe are so proud to be the front runners of the boutique hospitality space, which Sri Lanka has come to be known for,\u201d Dawoodbhoy noted in a statement issued to mark the anniversary."
