    "md5": "0cb3d774ec9b60c4a0683f6899cfab79",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1870,
    "chunk_text": "Colombo inflation rises to 8.3%\n\nFood inflation edges up to 8.8%; non-food inflation reaches 8.0%\n\nNon-food items account for nearly two-thirds of annual price increase\n\nColombo\u2019s annual inflation accelerated to 8.3 percent in October, from 8.0 percent in September, with higher transport, housing and food costs adding to the household expenditure, according to the Census and Statistics Department.\n\nThe increase followed two consecutive months at 8.0 percent and took inflation to its highest level so far in 2026. Annual inflation stood at 2.3 percent in January, fell to 1.6 percent in February and has risen each month since March, apart from September\u2019s pause.\n\nThe non-food items accounted for nearly two-thirds of October\u2019s annual price increase, underscoring the pressure from expenditure beyond the food basket. Transport alone contributed 2.56 percentage points to headline inflation, while housing, water, electricity, gas and other fuels added 1.22 percentage points.\n\nFood inflation edged up to 8.8 percent, from 8.7 percent in September, while non-food inflation increased to 8.0 percent, from 7.7 percent.\n\nThe Colombo Consumer Price Index (CCPI), compiled on a base of 2021=100, rose to 209.9 in October, from 209.1 in September. The Census and Statistics Department reported a monthly increase of 0.35 percent, equivalent to an additional Rs.666.63 in the expenditure value of the representative market basket.\n\nThe monthly increase was driven overwhelmingly by the non-food items, which added 0.33 percentage points to the overall movement. Food contributed just 0.02 percentage points, as the declines in several staples and protein sources largely offset the increases in the fresh produce.\n\nRestaurants and hotels made the largest contribution to the monthly rise, at 0.17 percentage points, followed by transport, at 0.13 percentage points."
  },
