    "chunk_id": "2026-10-31-dailymirrorlk-82b94cde-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "7c7553ae86eb28a91104671f72ba8eee",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1421,
    "chunk_text": "However, in the end, the regular bureaucratic and regulatory bottlenecks and protracted inter-departmental wrangling ( On this occasion, between the Ministry of Finance, Port authorities and Inland Revenue Department) seemed to have hit the nail in the coffin.\n\nFollowing the abandonment of the project, the Sri Lanka Port Authority has announced plans to re-tender a 150,000-square-meter logistics hub under a new Public-Private Partnership.\u00a0 One might argue the new PPP would offer a greater level playing field for international investors, and reposition the project towards more holistic international maritime supply chains, leveraging Colombo Port\u2019s existing strength as a transhipment hub. Seventy per cent of Colombo Port\u2019s transhipment volume is destined for India ( which accounts for 30 per cent of India\u2019s transhipment volume). With the emergence of rival ports such as Vizhinjam, one might argue that the cautious approach would be to disentangle from potentially geopolitical considerations that might constrain Indian reliance on Colombo.\n\nHowever, Sri Lankans who have a habit of reading too much into geopolitics fail to account for the primary contributory factor for recurrent deadlocks and abandonment of high-profile investment projects. They are all domestic: Institutional incompetence, regulatory bottlenecks and ad hoc policy changes have seriously dented Sri Lanka\u2019s investment climate."
  },
  {
