    "md5": "5c3c75c2d2aeabe8d058d4613a7341be",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1772,
    "chunk_text": "\"She told me I was better off going home. There was nothing holding me there so I agreed to go.\"\n\nBut when she turned up at the airport with an Air New Zealand ticket to Sri Lanka in hand, she was told she couldn't board the flight without a New Zealand transit visa.\n\nIn one of its rejections, New Zealand suggested she explore other routes. But both the US state of Hawaii and the French Polynesian island of Tahiti told her she would need to provide biometric information before any visa could be processed - and that could only be done in person in New Zealand.\n\nHer latest application to New Zealand, which included all the details of the alternative routes she'd been trying, as well as her son's upcoming wedding and mother's failing health, was also rejected.\n\n\"I have lost so much money on tickets. For almost four years I have been just applying for visa after visa with nothing working out,\" she says, breaking down.\n\nOn a collection of islands known for their sweeping sandy beaches and rugged volcanic peaks, just four hours from Auckland, Ranasingha has been an involuntary guest.\n\nShe's luckier than others in her position, she says, with her hotel letting her stay free of charge in its staff accommodation and friendly locals providing her with food and clothing.\n\nOne of the hotel directors even gave her a sewing machine, she says, enabling her to make a small income.\n\n\"My mother always tells me I must have done something good, for me to be in a place like this where people have been so kind to me,\" she tells the BBC.\n\nRanasingha says the stress is impacting her health. \"I am scared something is going to happen to me,\" she says.\n\n\"I don't know what her solution is, to be honest,\" says Auckland-based immigration law specialist Alastair McClymont."
  },
