    "md5": "d531c6649ef712aee6fc15978e3eacb8",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1809,
    "chunk_text": "Crypto firm Binance covers 90% of losses after $6.3bn hack\n\nBBC - Binance, the world's largest cryptocurrency exchange, says it has covered 90% of the $6.3bn (\u00a34.7bn) assets stolen in a hack on Thursday.\n\nThe remaining 10% may take longer to source in the required amounts, the company added.\n\nBinance has consistently pledged to cover 100% of the losses from what is suspected to be crypto's biggest hack on record and said it has set aside more than $6.34bn in company funds to do so.\n\n\"Binance will absorb the financial impact of these stolen funds, not our users,\" the company said in a statement on Saturday.\n\nThe Federal Bureau of Investigation (FBI) said on Saturday that North Korea was responsible for the hack.\n\nTwo analytics firms previously told the BBC they had robust intelligence pointing to North Korean state-backed hacking unit the Lazarus Group, also known as TraderTraitor - which was specifically named by the FBI - as the likely culprits.\n\nBinance said it had replenished the stolen assets using various funds and its own corporate treasury.\n\nAll holdings of the cryptocurrencies bitcoin, ethereum and Tether are covered, along with seven other widely-traded crypto assets, the company said.\n\nThe company said its next goal is resuming withdrawal services for users, saying it remained on track to reopen withdrawals on Sunday 1 November and would provide a further update once details were confirmed.\n\nThe hack is believed to be the largest in history, eclipsing the record of a $1.5bn heist on the Bybit exchange in 2025 - for which the FBI also said North Korea was responsible.\n\nBinance's initial investigation suggested an engineer's laptop was compromised, which allowed access to unauthorised withdrawals, but a later update said they had uncovered \"additional points of breach\"."
  },
