    "chunk_size_bytes": 1775,
    "chunk_text": "\u2018As I was accessing the e-visa on the side, this lady from behind the desk raised her voice so the entire area could hear and said: \u201cHurry up! Hurry up! You have to do this quickly, otherwise I\u2019m going to close boarding. Hurry up and get this done. Step aside now.\u201d Sangakkara asked for more time to access the e-visas, only to be told once again to hurry up, at which point he told the lady she was being \u2018very rude\u2019.\n\nHe says she responded by saying he was being \u2018argumentative and aggressive\u2019, adding: \u2018I\u2019m going to treat you as a threat to other passengers on board and not board you.\u2019\n\nAt this point, Sangakkara\u2019s wife, Yehali, said: \u2018You do realise what you said is so offensive, right? We are trying to access our passports, and you are shouting at us here in front of all these people. And you just made a very offensive comment. We\u2019ve had such a good trip to Dublin, met such nice people. The only rude person we have met is you.\u2019\n\nThe Sangakkaras were eventually allowed to board, but not before Kumar asked for the lady\u2019s name so he could register a formal complaint. He says she replied: \u2018I don\u2019t have to give you my name. And if you keep asking for this, I\u2019m not going to board you because I will consider you a threat.\n\n\u2018Then my wife explained step by step why we were in Ireland, all of which she didn\u2019t even have to do. But she was very calm, very measured, and she explained to her how rude it was, how offensive her remark was, because to us, I would take that as a very racist comment.\n\n\u2018Ryanair said I had refused to show my visa, which is completely untrue. I would completely understand if I had been an a** and refused, but I never play the race card for the sake of it, and this was an offensive comment.\u2019"
