    "md5": "eda541dbccdfee7b053b97f3c3504e4e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2006,
    "chunk_text": "Shops looted as German cost of living protests turn violent\n\nBBC - Rioters looted shops in central Frankfurt late on Saturday as clashes erupted in German cities over the cost of living.\n\nBesides Frankfurt - Germany's financial hub - there were incidents of violence in Berlin, Cologne and Hamburg, with police saying they used water cannon to disperse protesters targeting emergency workers with fireworks, stones and bottles.\n\nThousands joined largely peaceful demonstrations earlier in the day. They were organised by the campaign group Gen Z regelt - meaning \"Gen Z takes charge\" - to protest against underfunding in schools, the high cost of living, public spending cuts and military service.\n\nThe unrest in Germany follows similar youth-led mass protests in France, where more than 320,000 people also demonstrated on Saturday.\n\nA large section of Frankfurt's main shopping street was closed off by police. A police spokesperson said many arrests were made in the city.\n\nAuthorities in Frankfurt, Munich and Berlin reported as many as 18,000 protesters, roughly half of what the organisers had estimated on their website. German media say tens of thousands protested in German cities on Saturday.\n\nGen Z regelt spokesperson Lina Friedrich said the demonstrations were \"to fight for peace and freedom, rather than for war and coercion\".\n\nShe said: \"What matters most to us is better support for the health and education systems. We are against compulsory military service and we are in favour of a fairer distribution of the burden.\"\n\nOn Saturday night Frankfurt police said \"violent riots\" were occurring in the city centre and that special police units had been deployed. The windows of several shops had been smashed and molotov cocktails were thrown at emergency services, police said.\n\nOfficials said the windows of a gun shop were smashed and that they had seized two weapons after arresting a suspect. People also broke into other shops, stealing trainers and other items, local media reported."
