    "md5": "5f889f806dc200d7987ea38e1ec684b9",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1783,
    "chunk_text": "Bird\u2019s-eye view: Economy stable and growing. Worm\u2019s-eye view: Not so rosy\n\nThe private sector, widely regarded as the engine of growth in Sri Lanka, contributes more than three-quarters of the country\u2019s national wealth. Yet,\u00a0 unresolved governance failures in some businesses leave workers and consumers vulnerable. Two examples illustrate the problem: the exploitation of young workers by small garment factories operating outside Colombo, and the defrauding of customers by corrupt insurance agents\n\nMultilateral lenders such as the International Monetary Fund (IMF) and the World Bank continue to commend Sri Lanka\u2019s recovery from the devastating crisis of 2022. From their macroeconomic\u2014or bird\u2019s-eye\u2014view, the picture looks encouraging. Budgetary numbers are improving, reforms are progressing, inflation remains in single digits despite recently exceeding the Central Bank\u2019s upper limit, foreign reserves are rising, and economic growth of 5% suggests a firm recovery. To international donors and local policymakers, the message is reassuring: the economy is stable and growing.\n\nLower the gaze to the ground, however, and the worm\u2019s-eye view is strikingly different. Macroeconomic indicators cannot capture the hardships endured by ordinary people. Economics is not merely about aggregates; it is about human lives. Seen from above, a tree may display a flourishing green canopy. Examined leaf by leaf, it may reveal diseases quietly consuming it.\n\nThis problem is not new. In ancient Lanka, courtiers often assured kings that their subjects were content while concealing their hardships. Wise rulers therefore travelled through villages at night in disguise, discovering conditions for themselves and addressing grievances before they threatened the realm."
  },
