    "chunk_size_bytes": 1376,
    "chunk_text": "unresolved governance failures in some businesses leave workers and consumers vulnerable. Two examples illustrate the problem: the exploitation of young workers by small garment factories operating outside Colombo, and the defrauding of customers by corrupt insurance agents.\n\nThe second case concerns corrupt insurance agents at major insurance companies who exploit the three-month credit window available when arranging a policy. This practice is particularly common in motor-vehicle insurance. A vehicle owner pays the premium to an agent, but instead of remitting it to the insurer, the agent pockets the money. The agent then issues documentation that appears to provide cover for a full year, although the policy is valid for only three months. The fraud usually comes to light only after the vehicle is involved in an accident and the owner submits a claim. By then, the policy has lapsed. Although insurance companies may be aware of this misconduct, they sometimes fail to act because the offenders are high-performing \u201cgolden agents\u201d.\n\nThese cases demonstrate how poor governance can persist in both small and large private businesses. In each instance, commercial success shields misconduct, while workers and customers bear the cost. Such abuses may remain invisible in national accounts, but at ground level they cause severe financial and personal hardship."
  },
