    "md5": "5968d4bcd3c7c39a3c6c074d3832ceb4",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1931,
    "chunk_text": "These cases demonstrate how poor governance can persist in both small and large private businesses. In each instance, commercial success shields misconduct, while workers and customers bear the cost. Such abuses may remain invisible in national accounts, but at ground level they cause severe financial and personal hardship.\n\nIn some areas, the Government sector performs remarkably well. Sri Lanka Customs and the Inland Revenue Department, for example, have met revenue targets assigned by the budget, demonstrating that public institutions can deliver when goals, systems, and accountability are clear. Elsewhere, however, failures and inefficiencies cause immense hardship. Sri Lanka is rightly proud of maintaining free healthcare and education for decades. These services have expanded opportunity and social welfare, yet weaknesses in their day-to-day delivery provoke widespread public outcry. Citizens value the principle of universal provision but suffer when the institutions established to honour that principle cannot provide timely, dependable service.\n\nIn Government hospitals, shortages of basic facilities leave patients distressed. A patient may receive an appointment for critical surgery, make the necessary family and financial arrangements, and enter a major hospital expecting treatment. Only after admission may the operation be postponed because a surgeon, essential medicine, or vital machine is unavailable. The delay is not merely administrative: it prolongs illness, increases uncertainty, and may impose additional travel and living costs on the family. Wards are frequently overcrowded, beds are scarce, and some patients remain standing for hours while waiting for attention. Doctors, nurses, and other staff may work under severe pressure, but the citizen experiences the institution as a whole. A service intended to protect people at their most vulnerable can therefore add pain and uncertainty."
