    "chunk_size_bytes": 1540,
    "chunk_text": "In Government hospitals, shortages of basic facilities leave patients distressed. A patient may receive an appointment for critical surgery, make the necessary family and financial arrangements, and enter a major hospital expecting treatment. Only after admission may the operation be postponed because a surgeon, essential medicine, or vital machine is unavailable. The delay is not merely administrative: it prolongs illness, increases uncertainty, and may impose additional travel and living costs on the family. Wards are frequently overcrowded, beds are scarce, and some patients remain standing for hours while waiting for attention. Doctors, nurses, and other staff may work under severe pressure, but the citizen experiences the institution as a whole. A service intended to protect people at their most vulnerable can therefore add pain and uncertainty.\n\nAdministrative delays compound the burden. Vehicle owners may wait 15 months after registration for official number plates, despite having completed the required procedures and payments. Until the plates arrive, they face uncertainty in using or transferring their vehicles. Drivers who pass the prescribed tests may likewise fail to receive permanent licences because cards are unavailable. Temporary arrangements do not remove the inconvenience or anxiety caused by the State\u2019s inability to issue a basic document. These may seem minor problems from above, but at ground level they consume time, disrupt daily life, and expose law-abiding citizens to needless uncertainty."
