    "chunk_text": "Administrative delays compound the burden. Vehicle owners may wait 15 months after registration for official number plates, despite having completed the required procedures and payments. Until the plates arrive, they face uncertainty in using or transferring their vehicles. Drivers who pass the prescribed tests may likewise fail to receive permanent licences because cards are unavailable. Temporary arrangements do not remove the inconvenience or anxiety caused by the State\u2019s inability to issue a basic document. These may seem minor problems from above, but at ground level they consume time, disrupt daily life, and expose law-abiding citizens to needless uncertainty.\n\nThese failures inflict pain that aggregate statistics cannot measure. Citizens judge the State by whether a hospital performs a scheduled operation, a school teaches effectively, a department issues an essential document promptly, and the police respond when help is sought. When basic services fail, the worm\u2019s-eye view remains far less reassuring than the bird\u2019s-eye view.\n\nEven well-intentioned regulations can backfire when they ignore behaviour at ground level. Sri Lanka introduced a Quick Response (QR) code to ration petrol and diesel during a period of scarcity, limiting most motorcars to 25 litres a week. An odd-and-even date rule tied eligibility to registration numbers and was intended to control queues and distribute limited supplies fairly. Yet many motorists found the quota inadequate for commuting and essential travel. The date restriction created a further difficulty: an empty tank on the wrong day could leave a driver stranded even when fuel was available. A rule designed at the centre for orderly allocation thus created powerful incentives for evasion at the pump."
