    "chunk_text": "Even well-intentioned regulations can backfire when they ignore behaviour at ground level. Sri Lanka introduced a Quick Response (QR) code to ration petrol and diesel during a period of scarcity, limiting most motorcars to 25 litres a week. An odd-and-even date rule tied eligibility to registration numbers and was intended to control queues and distribute limited supplies fairly. Yet many motorists found the quota inadequate for commuting and essential travel. The date restriction created a further difficulty: an empty tank on the wrong day could leave a driver stranded even when fuel was available. A rule designed at the centre for orderly allocation thus created powerful incentives for evasion at the pump.\n\nA mechanism intended to ensure equity thus bred ground-level corruption. Money that should have entered the revenues of State or private petroleum companies flowed instead into individual pockets. Honest motorists either accepted inadequate supplies or paid bribes to overcome the restrictions, while those with connections or cash obtained more. Ordinary citizens were pushed towards misconduct merely to meet transport needs, widening the gap between clean digital models and compromised daily reality.\n\nTaken together, these ground-level realities reveal a fractured recovery. The bird\u2019s-eye view of lenders and policymakers shows fiscal discipline, single-digit inflation, rising reserves, and renewed growth. The worm\u2019s-eye view shows ordinary citizens squeezed by private corruption, public-sector failure, arbitrary regulation, and financial exclusion."
