    "md5": "a887da624cf6330c69df7dcce89693d6",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1990,
    "chunk_text": "Taken together, these ground-level realities reveal a fractured recovery. The bird\u2019s-eye view of lenders and policymakers shows fiscal discipline, single-digit inflation, rising reserves, and renewed growth. The worm\u2019s-eye view shows ordinary citizens squeezed by private corruption, public-sector failure, arbitrary regulation, and financial exclusion.\n\nThat convenience carries ruinous terms. Loans may have maturities as short as 100 days, with interest collected daily and amounting to as much as 10% a month. Daily collection makes each payment appear manageable, but the cumulative charge absorbs a large share of the borrower\u2019s earnings. At maturity, the principal is commonly rolled over on the same terms because the borrower has been unable to save enough to repay it. A temporary cash shortage is thereby converted into perpetual debt, and income that should sustain a family or expand a microenterprise is transferred continuously to the lender.\n\nThe exploitation thrives in the absence of strict, modern usury laws and effective enforcement. Even the historic Ultra Duplum rule\u2014which limits accumulated interest to the original principal\u2014is bypassed or poorly enforced under the century-old Money Lending Ordinance and newer microfinance regulation to be enforced under the recently enacted Microfinance Regulatory Authority Act. Vulnerable borrowers rarely possess the knowledge, records, time, or resources needed to challenge abusive terms. The resulting anger is widespread in rural and urban communities. As the economics writer Yadamini Gunawardena observed by coining the term \u201cThe 5km Blind Spot\u201d, such exploitation occurs within five kilometres of Colombo\u2019s skyscraper-filled financial district\u2014a systemic failure under policymakers\u2019 noses. Financial inclusion cannot be measured only by the number of bank branches, accounts, or digital applications; it must also be judged by whether citizens can obtain safe, affordable credit when they need it."
  },
