    "md5": "91fe448a79e8fd8f1ec3880ff5b095a2",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1896,
    "chunk_text": "Organised Crime Bill violates rights standards: HRW\n\nRepealing the PTA would fulfill a key commitment that Sri Lanka made to the European Union, in exchange for tariff-free access to the EU market under its Generalised Scheme of Preferences Plus (GSP+) program. However, the Prevention of Organised Crimes Bill preserves the terrorism act\u2019s abusive features, further weakening Sri Lanka\u2019s compliance with its GSP+ human rights commitments\n\nThe Sri Lankan Government\u2019s Prevention of Organised Crimes Bill, submitted to Parliament on 20 October, 2026, risks enabling arbitrary detention, torture, and other rights violations, Human Rights Watch said. The proposed law, alongside the Protection of the State from Terrorism Bill submitted on 6 October, reproduces abusive provisions in existing counterterrorism laws that would allow continuing abuses that the Government had pledged to end.\n\nPresident Anura Kumara Dissanayake\u2019s National People\u2019s Power alliance pledged in its 2024 election manifesto to abolish all \u201coppressive\u201d laws and ensure \u201ccivil rights of people in all parts of the country.\u201d In particular, the Dissanayake Government\u2014like its predecessors\u2014pledged to repeal the notorious Prevention of Terrorism Act (PTA). Both bills presented in October replicate some of the worst aspects of the PTA, shifting abusive provisions to create parallel legal structures outside existing criminal law and without basic safeguards.\n\nHuman Rights Watch Asia Director Elaine Pearson said: \u201cSri Lanka\u2019s organised crime bill would enable prolonged detention without adequate judicial oversight or evidence of criminal conduct, and could encourage torture by allowing convictions based on confessions obtained by the Police. If this law is adopted in its current form, it risks unleashing serious rights violations just as previous similarly draconian legislation has done.\u201d"
