    "chunk_text": "Human Rights Watch Asia Director Elaine Pearson said: \u201cSri Lanka\u2019s organised crime bill would enable prolonged detention without adequate judicial oversight or evidence of criminal conduct, and could encourage torture by allowing convictions based on confessions obtained by the Police. If this law is adopted in its current form, it risks unleashing serious rights violations just as previous similarly draconian legislation has done.\u201d\n\nThe bill defines \u201cserious crime\u201d as any offense punishable with at least four years in prison and lists a large number of offenses that fall under other laws, placing a wide variety of alleged criminal activity within its extraordinary authority. It defines an \u201corganised criminal group\u201d as three or more people acting in concert with the aim of committing one or more serious crimes to obtain, directly or indirectly, a financial or other material benefit.\n\nCourts may consider whether a person is a member of an organised criminal group based on factors including physical or virtual meetings with known members; their style of dress, hand signs, language or tattoos; association with known members of the group; and habitually visiting premises known to be used by the group. Such provisions risk criminalising association rather than conduct, allowing the authorities to detain a person without any evidence of criminal activity.\n\nThe bill would allow the Public Security Ministry Secretary\u00a0 to issue a detention order for two months at a time, for up to a year, without giving the magistrate discretion to set the detention order aside. Bail may be granted only by the High Court, with suspects accused of a serious crime only released on bail in exceptional circumstances."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-02-dailyftlk-b89fbf4d",
    "num": "dailyftlk-b89fbf4d",
    "date_str": "2026-11-02",
    "description": "Organised Crime Bill violates rights standards: HRW",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/opinion/Organised-Crime-Bill-violates-rights-standards-HRW/14-799476",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1793570913.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-02-dailyftlk-b89fbf4d-0002",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "f9c1d2bb91089c962113de35c2f98844",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1814,
    "chunk_text": "The bill would allow the Public Security Ministry Secretary\u00a0 to issue a detention order for two months at a time, for up to a year, without giving the magistrate discretion to set the detention order aside. Bail may be granted only by the High Court, with suspects accused of a serious crime only released on bail in exceptional circumstances.\n\nThe bill provides police with broad surveillance authority, including to intercept and record communications, carry out forensic extraction of data from electronic devices, and access data from telecommunications companies and online services, through court orders.\n\nThe Government did not appear to conduct consultations while drafting the bill. The Government should withdraw the bill, undertake a genuine, open, and inclusive consultation, ensure legislation with robust safeguards to prevent arbitrary detention, torture, and ill treatment, and ensure judicial oversight and due process, in compliance with the Government\u2019s international legal obligations\n\nThe Police could, even without a Courtorder, obtain any telecommunication, satellite, or digital service provider information relating to the identity of the owner or subscriber, calls, and the tower location, of any device, sweeping powers to expose personal communications and information and infringe rights, including of privacy and assembly. Sri Lankan law enforcement and security agencies have a long record of misusing surveillance to target human rights defenders and the families of victims of rights violations.\n\nThe organised crime bill also contains sections that could curtail freedom of expression by making it an offense to publish the name or anything that could identify a victim of crime. Further restrictions are placed on media reporting of court proceedings without court permission."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
