    "md5": "c80df590dd1310d8129af4b32f1747d2",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1753,
    "chunk_text": "Tamil leaders take anti-terror law fears to UK envoy\n\nTamil political leaders last week met British High Commissioner Andrew Patrick to convey concerns that the Government\u2019s proposed anti-terrorism and organised crime laws could be used against minority communities, with the envoy expressing deep concern over the anti-terrorism legislation, according to the Tamil Eelam Liberation Organization (TELO).\n\nTELO Leader and Democratic Tamil National Alliance (DTNA) MP Selvam Adaikkalanathan and TELO Vice President and Spokesperson and DTNA Deputy National Organiser Guruswamy Surendran discussed with the High Commissioner the risks posed to the country, and to minority communities in particular, by the Protection of the State from Terrorism Act (PSTA) and the Organised Crime Bill presented to Parliament by the Government.\n\nThe TELO leaders said the proposed laws could be used to suppress, or facilitate the arbitrary and targeted arrest of, those campaigning on long-standing issues, including the demand for a permanent political solution for Tamils in the Northern and Eastern Provinces, land grabbing, enforced disappearances, war crimes, human rights violations, the need for a justice mechanism and the repeated postponement of Provincial Council elections.\n\nThey noted that while UN Human Rights Council (UNHRC) reports had called for the repeal of the Prevention of Terrorism Act (PTA), the Government was instead attempting to introduce alternative legislation that posed a significant threat to Tamils and the country as a whole. They also said the continued delay in holding Provincial Council elections had left their regions without a provincial administrative structure, which in turn facilitated land grabbing by State institutions."
  },
